# Fixed 2026-11-20 (defect 2522's row 1, lane-signals), refused then as a
# known cost by the author's ruling B of that day; runs since 2026-11-23
# (panel 255's R1 and R2, lane-p255-impl, ratified the same day): a correct
# program saves a buffer, copies it, and its handler jumps out of itself
# through the copy; the signal over, it calls the function from far deeper.
# Why it runs: a jump reads one word of the buffer it jumps to, the one the
# C library installs next, and decodes the stack pointer it will land at, so
# the copy is followed for what it is: the jump leaves the handler, the
# handler's entry is dropped, and the later call is outside any signal.
# Route A, where the key is not verified, still refuses it.
# Why it was refused before: the runtime kept a record of each buffer a save
# filled and a copy had none, so a jump through it could not be told from
# one landing inside the handler.
# Symptom before 2026-11-23: the refusal, in words true of a jump it could
# not follow (40 runs of 40 on this Mac at `-O0` and `-O2`, 20 of 20 at
# `-O0` in Linux arm64, 2026-11-20). The refill case beside it stays refused.
extern "fixedbugs-2522-a-jump-out-of-a-handler-through-a-copied-buffer.h"
    function copied_install(sig: i32) -> i32
    function copied_escape(sig: i32) -> i32
    function copied_call(cb: (function(i64) -> i64)) -> i64

extern "signal.h"
    constant SIGUSR1: i32

function answer(n: i64) -> i64
    return n + 1

function main()
    print("installed ", copied_install(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("escaped ", copied_escape(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("called ", copied_call(cb: answer))
